  "query_preview": "..\n1) Unsettled weather will persist for the next 7 days at least. \nGreatest chance for rainfall will be over the mountains and\nadjacent foothills. \n\nSub-tropical high pressure remains parked over the western\nAtlantic  through this weekend, although it is gradually \nsquashed as the upper level trough axis over the central states \nshifts east. By mid week, we will be further ensconced within \nthe mean trough as the main storm track builds south. There will\nbe no cold frontal passage anytime soon to scour at high \nmoisture content over the Mid Atlantic. Through the weekend and \nall of next week, expect daily showers and thunderstorms. The \nmain forcing mechanisms, especially through Monday, will be \ndifferential heating and interaction of the southerly moisture \nfetch with the mountains and foothills. For this weekend \nhowever, Saturday looks to have the highest chance for more \nwidespread convection, with a surface trough in the region. \nOverall though, with weak low level lapse rates and shear, \nstorms should be disorganized and on the weaker side. Main \nconcern will be flash flooding, and with slow storm movement, \nany locations that receive multiple rounds of showers/storms may\nexperience flash flooding. \n\nAs high pressure off the coast dips in latitude, winds gradually \nveer with more of a westerly component developing beginning \nSunday into next week. So while we will still have heavy \nrainfall and the threat for flash flooding, the winds being more\nparallel to the terrain helps lower that threat somewhat. The \nsevere threat, however, has the potential to increase, with a \nhigher chance for a few strong mid level waves moving our way \nbeginning Tuesday and beyond, after a mostly down day as far as \nrain on Monday. So, the threat for damaging wet microbursts \nbegins creeping back up Tuesday onward.\n\nHigh temperatures warm up slightly going into next week, with\nupper 70s to upper 80s for the mountains and foothills, and \nmid 80s to mid 90s for the Piedmont. Overnight lows will be \nabout 3 to 5 degrees above normal with plenty of cloud cover and\nmoisture around.",
